C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Engineering Systems Analysis
      Example 2.1 from Bishop Et Al Text (https://digitalcommons.usu.edu/ecstatic_all/76/)
      Modifies Example to add a labor constraint
       
      THE PROBLEM:
       
      An manufacturer can produce two types of vehicles: coups and minivans.  Data are as fol-lows:
       
      Seasonal Resource
      Inputs or Profit        Vehicles        Resource
      Availability
              Coups        Minivans
      Metal        1000 lbs/vehicle        2000 lbs/vehicle      4000000 lbs/year
      Circut Boards        4 circut boards/vehicle        3 circut boards/vehicle               12000 circut boards/year
      Labor         5 days/vehicle        2.5 days/vehicle              17,500 days/year
      Profit/vehicle        $6000        $7000
       
                      Determine the optimal production for the two vehicles.
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      Created:
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 15, 2015
       
      Modified:
      Samuel T. Torgersen-Gonzalez
      s.torgersen-gonzalez@usu.edu
      September 21, 2026
  34   
  35  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  36  SETS vhcl vehicles produced /Coups, Minivans/
  37       res resources /Metal, CircutBoards, Labor/;
  38   
  39  * 2. DEFINE input data
  40  PARAMETERS
  41     c(vhcl) Objective function coefficients ($ per vehicle)
  42           /Coups 6000,
  43          Minivans 7000 /
  44   
  45     b(res) Right hand constraint values (per resource)
  46            /Metal 4000000,
  47             CircutBoards  12000,
  48             Labor  17500/;
  49   
  50  TABLE A(vhcl,res) Left hand side constraint coefficients
  51                   Metal    CircutBoards   Labor
  52   Coups           1000     4              5
  53   Minivans        2000     3              2.5;
  54   
  55   
  56  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  57  VARIABLES X(vhcl) vehicles produced (Number)
  58            VPROFIT  total profit ($);
  59   
  60  * Non-negativity constraints
  61  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  62   
  63  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  64  EQUATIONS
  65     PROFIT Total profit ($) and objective function value
  66     RES_CONSTRAIN(res) Resource Constraints;
  67   
  68  PROFIT..                 VPROFIT =E= SUM(vhcl, c(vhcl)*X(vhcl));
  69  RES_CONSTRAIN(res) ..    SUM(vhcl, A(vhcl,res)*X(vhcl)) =L= b(res);
  70   
  71   
  72  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  73  MODEL PRODUCTING /PROFIT, RES_CONSTRAIN/;
  74  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
  75  *MODEL PRODUCTING /ALL/;
  76   
  77   
  78  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  79  * Solve the PRODUCTING model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
  80  *     to maximize VPROFIT
  81  SOLVE PRODUCTING USING LP MAXIMIZING VPROFIT;
  82   
  83   
  84  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


